{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-35689" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $B={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]}$ and $C={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}$ be bases of ${R}^{2}.$ Find the change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072000/state.json" "file_sha256" "24e7a7dbd7f6830b802419389c60333ec7d754a2cf7cfcb74e11a02de144e442"} {"question" "q-35689" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $C$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ relative to the basis $B.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -3 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the right-hand side, we obtain the change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{C\\to B}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072000/state.json" "file_sha256" "24e7a7dbd7f6830b802419389c60333ec7d754a2cf7cfcb74e11a02de144e442"} {"question" "q-35689" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072000/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d2e0ffdc16798a26f180bd0f87d296315db48a00e5b663463a59c10d3f0a58a0"} {"question" "q-35689" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072000/state.json" "file_sha256" "24e7a7dbd7f6830b802419389c60333ec7d754a2cf7cfcb74e11a02de144e442"} {"question" "q-35689" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -9\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072000/state.json" "file_sha256" "24e7a7dbd7f6830b802419389c60333ec7d754a2cf7cfcb74e11a02de144e442"} {"question" "q-35689" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072000/state.json" "file_sha256" "24e7a7dbd7f6830b802419389c60333ec7d754a2cf7cfcb74e11a02de144e442"} {"question" "q-81847" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and let $S$ be the standard basis of ${R}^{2}.$ \nFind the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{B\\to S}$ from $B$ to $S$ if \n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 11\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072000/state.json" "file_sha256" "24e7a7dbd7f6830b802419389c60333ec7d754a2cf7cfcb74e11a02de144e442"} {"question" "q-81847" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall  that the standard basis of ${R}^{2}$ consists of the vectors ${e}_{1}$ and ${e}_{2},$ given by\n\n$$\n{e}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{e}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe components of the vectors of $B$ are just the coordinates relative to the standard basis:\n\n$$\n[{b}_{1}]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,[{b}_{2}]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 11\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is a matrix whose respective columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $S.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 11\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072000/state.json" "file_sha256" "24e7a7dbd7f6830b802419389c60333ec7d754a2cf7cfcb74e11a02de144e442"} {"question" "q-81847" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072000/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d2e0ffdc16798a26f180bd0f87d296315db48a00e5b663463a59c10d3f0a58a0"} {"question" "q-81847" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072000/state.json" "file_sha256" "24e7a7dbd7f6830b802419389c60333ec7d754a2cf7cfcb74e11a02de144e442"} {"question" "q-81847" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ 11 & 5\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 11\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 11\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ 11 & 3\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}5 & 11 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072000/state.json" "file_sha256" "24e7a7dbd7f6830b802419389c60333ec7d754a2cf7cfcb74e11a02de144e442"} {"question" "q-81847" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 11\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072000/state.json" "file_sha256" "24e7a7dbd7f6830b802419389c60333ec7d754a2cf7cfcb74e11a02de144e442"} {"question" "q-81855" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $B$ to the basis $C$ is given by \n${P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -7 \\\\ 3 & -5\\end{aligned}].$\nFind the change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B$  ." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072000/state.json" "file_sha256" "24e7a7dbd7f6830b802419389c60333ec7d754a2cf7cfcb74e11a02de144e442"} {"question" "q-81855" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B$ is the inverse of the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$ Therefore, we simply need to find the inverse of the given matrix.\n\nComputing the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{C\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}4 & -7 \\\\ 3 & -5\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\cdot (-5)-(-7)\\cdot 3}[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 7 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-5 & 7 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 7 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072000/state.json" "file_sha256" "24e7a7dbd7f6830b802419389c60333ec7d754a2cf7cfcb74e11a02de144e442"} {"question" "q-81855" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072000/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d2e0ffdc16798a26f180bd0f87d296315db48a00e5b663463a59c10d3f0a58a0"} {"question" "q-81855" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072000/state.json" "file_sha256" "24e7a7dbd7f6830b802419389c60333ec7d754a2cf7cfcb74e11a02de144e442"} {"question" "q-81855" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -3 \\\\ -7 & 4\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -7 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 7 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ 7 & -5\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}4 & 7 \\\\ -3 & -5\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072000/state.json" "file_sha256" "24e7a7dbd7f6830b802419389c60333ec7d754a2cf7cfcb74e11a02de144e442"} {"question" "q-81855" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 7 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072000/state.json" "file_sha256" "24e7a7dbd7f6830b802419389c60333ec7d754a2cf7cfcb74e11a02de144e442"} {"question" "q-113105" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$ be bases of ${R}^{2}.$ If ${b}_{1}=2{c}_{2}-2{c}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}={c}_{1}-2{c}_{2},$ find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{B\\to C}$ from $B$ to $C.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072000/state.json" "file_sha256" "24e7a7dbd7f6830b802419389c60333ec7d754a2cf7cfcb74e11a02de144e442"} {"question" "q-113105" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The coordinates of the vectors in $B$ relative to the basis $C$ are\n\n$$\n[{b}_{1}]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,[{b}_{2}]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose respective columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $C.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072000/state.json" "file_sha256" "24e7a7dbd7f6830b802419389c60333ec7d754a2cf7cfcb74e11a02de144e442"} {"question" "q-113105" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072000/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d2e0ffdc16798a26f180bd0f87d296315db48a00e5b663463a59c10d3f0a58a0"} {"question" "q-113105" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072000/state.json" "file_sha256" "24e7a7dbd7f6830b802419389c60333ec7d754a2cf7cfcb74e11a02de144e442"} {"question" "q-113105" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072000/state.json" "file_sha256" "24e7a7dbd7f6830b802419389c60333ec7d754a2cf7cfcb74e11a02de144e442"} {"question" "q-113105" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072000/state.json" "file_sha256" "24e7a7dbd7f6830b802419389c60333ec7d754a2cf7cfcb74e11a02de144e442"} {"question" "q-113411" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $C$ to the basis $B$ is given by\n${P}_{C\\to B}=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].$ Find the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072000/state.json" "file_sha256" "24e7a7dbd7f6830b802419389c60333ec7d754a2cf7cfcb74e11a02de144e442"} {"question" "q-113411" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is the inverse of the change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B.$ Therefore, we simply need to find the inverse of the given matrix.\n\nComputing the inverse of ${P}_{C\\to B}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C} & ={P}_{C\\to B}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\cdot (-1)-(-2)\\cdot 1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =(-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & -1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072000/state.json" "file_sha256" "24e7a7dbd7f6830b802419389c60333ec7d754a2cf7cfcb74e11a02de144e442"} {"question" "q-113411" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072000/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d2e0ffdc16798a26f180bd0f87d296315db48a00e5b663463a59c10d3f0a58a0"} {"question" "q-113411" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072000/state.json" "file_sha256" "24e7a7dbd7f6830b802419389c60333ec7d754a2cf7cfcb74e11a02de144e442"} {"question" "q-113411" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & -2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & -2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & -2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & -1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & -2\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072000/state.json" "file_sha256" "24e7a7dbd7f6830b802419389c60333ec7d754a2cf7cfcb74e11a02de144e442"} {"question" "q-113411" "field" "selection" 